WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
(light music)

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.517
- [Narrator] This is
the giant garter snake.

00:00:04.517 --> 00:00:07.953
They're endemic to the central
valley wetlands of California

00:00:07.953 --> 00:00:10.289
and considered an aquatic species,

00:00:10.289 --> 00:00:12.397
but with the loss of
wetlands in the region,

00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:16.167
the garter snake is no longer
found in 98% of the areas

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.100
it once resided in.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.210
(light music)

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.785
Considered the largest of
the garter snake family,

00:00:23.785 --> 00:00:27.913
they can grow to be more
than five feet long.

00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:29.391
While they are venomous creatures,

00:00:29.391 --> 00:00:32.006
they pose no risk to humans.

00:00:32.006 --> 00:00:33.885
Their mild venom subdues its prey,

00:00:33.885 --> 00:00:37.280
which consists mainly of fish and frogs.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.613
These snakes are brown or olive-colored,

00:00:42.029 --> 00:00:44.183
but can easily be spotted
by their yellow stripes.

00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:46.587
They are secretive and
difficult to approach

00:00:46.587 --> 00:00:49.023
and are known to dive into
water before an observer

00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:50.523
can get too close.

00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:54.427
Conservation efforts to
create artificial wetlands

00:00:54.427 --> 00:00:58.897
are underway to try and restore
their dwindling populations.

00:00:58.897 --> 00:01:00.878
This is the giant garter snake.

00:01:00.878 --> 00:01:03.378
(light music)

